@cc [owner:spolu,label:api] v1-client-compatibility
The server MUST accept the unchanged dfs.v1 protocol and client. Public limits, read-version equality,
kernel caching/writeback, and unlink-to-ENOENT MUST retain v1 behavior. Shared listings MAY include
directly granted entries also visible in the main tree or through another granted ancestor.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:concurrency] distinct-object-states
New objects start at version 1. Every new externally visible existing-object state MUST use a distinct
token in the existing uint64 field. Clients MUST compare tokens only for equality; tokens need not
increase in commit order. Each object's token MUST change independently of unrelated files.
Independent servers and restarts MUST NOT assign the same token to different existing-object states.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:architecture;concurrency] independent-server-writers
Independent dfs-server processes MUST be able to mutate the same workspace correctly. Cross-server
consistency MUST rely on FDB transactions, never an exclusive workspace owner, workspace writer lease,
or process-local lock/cache. Bounded local pending file edits MAY acknowledge RAM acceptance, but
publication MUST reapply semantic operations to current transactional state without replacing stale
parents, grants, namespace indexes, untouched bytes, or unspecified metadata.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:api;performance] unchanged-api-optimization
This optimization phase MUST preserve the exact existing API and v1 client, except for the approved
FUSE inode-cap increase to 1,000,000 and xattr filtering/caching project. Compound/bulk RPCs and new
client batching are out of scope. The approved server writeback changes acknowledgment and file
overwrite semantics only; optimizations MUST apply throughout the tree without root shortcuts.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:backend;concurrency;error-handling] server-writeback-boundary
Positioned file writes and file metadata updates MAY acknowledge bounded RAM acceptance and ignore
stale mutation versions. Local reads MUST include accepted pending edits; other servers see committed
FDB state. File fsync MUST durably publish its finite preceding prefix and report retained deferred
errors, including ambiguous outcomes. Later writes MUST NOT indefinitely extend this barrier.
Ordinary close MUST NOT add a durability barrier. Successful fsync MUST survive process loss;
unflushed acknowledgments MAY be lost. Create, rename, remove, grants, append, and directory updates
MUST retain synchronous durability and strict preconditions. Unlink MUST never resurrect an object.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:security] workspace-isolation
Session RPCs MUST derive workspace and grants from the authenticated session. Every workspace-owned
FDB key and ES document MUST be workspace scoped. Every file search MUST filter workspace explicitly;
routing alone MUST NOT authorize access. Backend errors MUST NOT expose credentials or hidden data.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:backend;concurrency] atomic-fdb-publication
Every filesystem publication MUST read live authorization and applicable namespace/version
preconditions in its committing FDB transaction with conflict tracking. Buffered file edits MUST
reapply their operations to current state rather than compare stale mutation versions. Each accepted operation MUST atomically change all
affected blocks, metadata, grants, and pending search jobs. Related reads MUST use one transaction read version.
Synchronous operations and fsync MUST await normal FDB commit. Fsync MUST propagate deferred errors
and check current server authority without waiting for ES.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:backend;performance] fdb-latency-tuning
Default client/server latency settings MUST match the pinned FDB native defaults. Nondefault latency
settings MUST require an explicit experimental override and MUST retain normal conflict resolution
and log synchronization before successful durability confirmation. Changes MUST be benchmarked with the
unchanged filesystem workload and independent writers.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:error-handling;concurrency] bounded-fdb-retries
Only FDB attempts known not to have committed MAY be retried automatically, within a bounded deadline.
Each retry MUST reauthorize; strict operations MUST retain original expected object versions, while
buffered file edits MUST reapply to current state. Ambiguous outcomes MUST return an error without
replay. Filesystem versions MUST remain per object, never per workspace.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:security;performance] live-search-authority
Each returned hit MUST match a live FDB object version and current session grants before any data is
exposed. Authorization memoization MUST be bounded, per search, and tied to one FDB read version.
An expired view MUST NOT be combined with a fresh view's decisions. No grants are materialized in ES.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:backend;concurrency] replay-safe-search
File mutations MUST enqueue coalescing work atomically; ancestor moves/grants MUST NOT fan out.
Indexing MUST NOT mix file versions. ES publication MUST precede token-checked completion in FDB.
Late ES requests MUST NOT overwrite newer indexed state or resurrect deletion tombstones.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:performance] bounded-storage-work
Buffers, scans, retries, RPC admission, caches, and index batches MUST be bounded. File blocks MUST
remain at most 65,536 bytes and arbitrary large files MUST NOT require whole-file buffers. FDB
transactions MUST respect size/lifetime limits without splitting atomic filesystem operations.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:rust] fallible-rust
Rust code MUST handle or propagate errors; unwrap() and expect() are forbidden. Unsafe code is
allowed only for the documented process-level FDB network lifetime boundary.
